A||N||4||Lewis||starter rebuild kit...will it solve my..||||1||
Z||000000||Lewis||06-28-2000||08:23 PM||||Will rebuild kit for a starter solve my starting problems.  Will not start till the car cools down abit.  When I try to start the car it is as if the battery is going dead.  I can get a kit for twenty dollars (canadian) but not sure if I should take the starter to the shop and have it rebuilt.  The kit is the cheaper choice.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||hugh||06-28-2000||09:00 PM||||If it only happens when your engineis hot it could be heat soak.One fix is to remove the starter solenoid from the starter and cut one coil from the spring that disengages the bendix gear.The spring is only to keep the gear out of the flywheel when the solenoid is not energized and break the motor circuit.<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||06-28-2000||09:09 PM||||I think the kit will not solve your problem.  It likely will contain only mechanical bits and perhaps brushes.  If it is dragging a known good battery down (cables should get hot-hot), then the windings are cooked and causing shorts.<BR>Its not the cost of the kit, but the pain of removing and replacing the starter, only to have to do it again.  Pull the starter and take it apart to see if something in the kit will repair any obvious deficency.  If not you can return the kit and get the starter. (make sure Canadian Tire et al have it in stock beforehand LOL), and good luck, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||Lewis||06-28-2000||09:27 PM||||Thanks guys..I think I will remove the starter and take it to the shop.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||06-28-2000||09:46 PM||||Before ripping your starter apart, check the ignition timing. If it turns over, but turns really slow, timing may be advanced too much causing the motor to fire against the starter. I assume the fuel injected motor will do that since timing is still set by the distributor position. ||||reg||1
